Set 1: Run-Loop Coalescing & Layout Thrashing

Mercor Primary Question: "In Ember.js applications handling high-frequency user actions, how does the Run Loop coordinate asynchronous updates, and how have you resolved layout thrashing caused by improper queue scheduling?"

Mercor Follow-up Probe: "Specifically, what happens under the hood if a developer reads DOM dimensions inside the afterRender queue while dispatching a tracked property mutation?"

Model Answer:

The Run Loop (via Backburner.js) batches DOM reads and writes across priority queues: sync, actions, router, render, afterRender, and destroy. Its goal is to prevent layout thrashing by ensuring DOM writes are executed together before subsequent reads.

Addressing the probe: If an engineer reads layout properties like element.offsetHeight in afterRender while updating an @tracked property, it forces the browser into a synchronous reflow (thrashing) to compute geometry, then schedules new work back into the actions or sync queue. This creates an uncoordinated re-render cascade inside a single animation frame.

Zoho Production Solution: In Zoho Books line-item tables, we eliminated this by using requestAnimationFrame or ResizeObserver callbacks outside the run-loop queue to measure sizes, deferring state updates via microtasks so measurement and mutation never interleave within the same tick.

Set 2: Auto-Tracking Mechanics vs. Classical Computed Properties

Mercor Primary Question: "Explain the fundamental shift from Ember Classic computed properties to Ember Octane tracked properties (@tracked). What happens in memory?"

Mercor Follow-up Probe: "Why did Classic computed properties cause memory retention issues in long-lived sessions compared to Octane's revision tag system?"

Model Answer:

Classic computed properties depended on string-based key paths (e.g., Ember.computed('items.@each.price')). Ember had to register explicit listeners on every single intermediate object in that path, building large dependency graph metadata that remained pinned in memory until explicitly detached.

Addressing the probe: In enterprise ERP workflows running all day, these listener chains prevented Garbage Collection of destroyed records if any child node remained referenced.

In Octane, @tracked uses a global revision counter and autotracking transaction tags. When a getter runs, Ember checks which @tracked tags were read and records their current revision number. No event listeners are attached to the models. A recalculation only happens if a tag's counter has incremented since the last render, cutting memory footprint by over 40% in large transactional tables.

Set 3: Glimmer VM Bytecode Execution vs. React VDOM Reconciliation

Mercor Primary Question: "Compare Glimmer VM's template compilation and wire-format execution with React's Virtual DOM diffing. Why is Glimmer particularly efficient for tabular enterprise interfaces?"

Mercor Follow-up Probe: "When a single cell updates in a 1,000-row table, what is the exact difference in runtime memory allocations between the two engines?"

Model Answer:

React creates an entire tree of plain JavaScript objects (Virtual DOM) representing the new state, comparing it recursively against the prior tree ($O(N)$ memory allocations relative to the subtree size).

Addressing the probe: Glimmer compiles templates into binary bytecode instructions at build time, strictly separating static HTML from dynamic expressions. When a single table cell updates, React allocates VDOM nodes across the row or table unless manually guarded with React.memo. Glimmer allocates zero DOM objects for the static structure—it skips straight to the dynamic bytecode address of that specific cell, reads the revision counter, and mutates the single text node via low-level DOM API. This keeps the JS heap flat during rapid voucher entries.

