Set 8: Financial Precision & Floating-Point Representation in JS

Mercor Primary Question: "Why is native JavaScript Number fundamentally dangerous for enterprise accounting applications, and how do you ensure zero calculation errors?"

Mercor Follow-up Probe: "How do you handle Banker's Rounding (Round-Half-To-Even) across different tax jurisdictions on the frontend?"

Model Answer:

JavaScript numbers are IEEE-754 double-precision floats. Values like 0.1 + 0.2 yield 0.30000000000000004 because base-10 fractional decimals cannot be represented exactly in binary floating-point.

Addressing the probe: Standard Math.round() rounds half up ($2.5 \to 3$), which introduces systematic statistical upward bias across large ledgers. International accounting standards (e.g., GAAP, IFRS) mandate Banker's Rounding (Round-Half-To-Even), where $2.5 \to 2$ and $3.5 \to 4$.

Implementation: We represent monetary values as immutable arbitrary-precision Decimals or integer minor units (cents/paise) based on currency metadata. All rounding operations accept explicit rounding modes and currency decimal rules (e.g., 2 decimals for USD, 3 for KWD, 0 for JPY) before data leaves calculation models.

