Set 42: Browser Layout Thrashing in Deep Tabular Views

Mercor Primary Question: "Explain what causes Layout Thrashing inside the browser engine, and how you have rewritten code to avoid it."

Mercor Follow-up Probe: "Identify the bug in this code snippet: items.forEach(el => el.style.width = container.offsetWidth + 'px');"

Model Answer:

Layout Thrashing occurs when JavaScript repeatedly interleaves reading geometric style properties (which forces the browser to recalculate layout) with writing style properties (which invalidates the layout).

Addressing the probe: In items.forEach(el => el.style.width = container.offsetWidth + 'px');, on every iteration the browser reads container.offsetWidth. Because the previous iteration modified el.style.width, the layout is dirty. The browser is forced to run a synchronous layout recalculation on every single item in the loop ($O(N)$ synchronous reflows).

The Fix: Decouple reads and writes:

const width = container.offsetWidth; // Single read
items.forEach(el => el.style.width = `${width}px`); // Batch writes


Set 43: HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 Multiplexing in Enterprise Asset Delivery

Mercor Primary Question: "How do HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 multiplexing affect asset bundling compared to legacy HTTP/1.1 practices?"

Mercor Follow-up Probe: "What is Head-of-Line (HoL) Blocking at the TCP transport layer, and how does HTTP/3 (QUIC) solve it?"

Model Answer:

Under HTTP/1.1, browsers were restricted to 6 TCP connections per origin, forcing developers to concatenate everything into massive single bundles. HTTP/2 introduced multiplexed streaming over a single TCP connection, allowing independent concurrent requests.

Addressing the probe: Under HTTP/2, all streams share one TCP connection. If a single packet is lost on the network, TCP halts all multiplexed streams while waiting for packet retransmission (TCP Head-of-Line Blocking).

HTTP/3 runs over QUIC (using UDP). Each stream is handled independently at the transport layer. A dropped packet on Stream A only pauses Stream A; Streams B, C, and D continue downloading without delay, ensuring stable asset loading on volatile mobile or enterprise networks.

