Set 45: Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs. WebSockets for Enterprise Notifications

Mercor Primary Question: "For delivering real-time notification feeds in Zoho Books, why would you select Server-Sent Events (SSE) over WebSockets?"

Mercor Follow-up Probe: "What happens to SSE connections when passing through corporate HTTP proxy servers with aggressive read timeouts?"

Model Answer:

WebSockets provide bidirectional full-duplex communication over a dedicated protocol. SSE provides unidirectional (server-to-client) streaming over standard HTTP.

Why SSE is Preferred for Notifications:

Operates over standard HTTP/2 without custom protocol handshakes.

Native browser auto-reconnection and message tracking via Last-Event-ID.

Lower server overhead since it reuses existing HTTP connection pooling.

Addressing the probe: Enterprise corporate proxies frequently kill idle HTTP connections after 30–60 seconds of inactivity. To keep SSE connections alive, the Java service must implement a scheduled heartbeat ping (e.g., sending a comment string :keepalive\n\n every 15 seconds), preventing intermediate proxies from terminating the stream.

